A||N||16||MotoFemme||"predictable" periodic stalling||||1||
Z||000000||MotoFemme||12-06-2001||04:14 PM||||Aidez-moi!  My friend has a 1988 4-cylinder Fiero which stalls intermittently, usually after about 10 minutes of driving.  Just before stalling, the idle gets very rough and sometimes the car bucks.  The car starts right up after stalling.  Sometimes it stalls again, but usually seems to resolve its issues and drives fine for the rest of the trip.  After stalling, sometimes we get a code 44.  We know its still getting sparkduring a stall, but it seems starved for gas.<P>Following advice in this forum, we have replaced the fuel filter, O2 sensor, coolant sensor, PCV, MAP, and IAC.  We've checked the EGR, the battery, the fuel pump, and for vacuum leaks. We also did the "tap test" of the ECM, all to no avail.<P>Now, instead of romantic drives around the lake, we have to cruise around in circles testing the latest "fix".  Can anyone help me?  I love working on cars, but if I have to spend another Sunday in the auto parts store, I'll go crazy.  Merci -||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pyric||12-06-2001||05:31 PM||||I had that same problem in another car except it would die every 15 minutes and then I could not start it for 30 minutes after.<P>Even though there was a spark, it turned out to be a bad distributor.  It would get hot and stop working.<P>Replaced it and it went away.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||12-06-2001||05:41 PM||||Don't think 88's have a dist.  Maybe a bad coil or ignition module?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||12-06-2001||05:49 PM||||I DO BELIVE IN WD-40<BR>WHILE i have never seen a duke I have fixed v6's by pulling apart wire conecters and spraying them with Wd-40 check grounds too.<BR>if dukes have tpi switch start there.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||12-06-2001||05:49 PM||||Does the stalling coincide with releasing the throttle?  A "gunked up" idle adjust motor may not respond fast enough to "save" the engine from stalling.  If this is the case, the fix is easy; clean the throttle body, particularly the idle bypass path.  I've had good luck with the aerosol spray cleaners.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Johnson's85SE||12-06-2001||06:03 PM||||I had the same problem. It was a cooked ignition module on my V6.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MotoFemme||12-07-2001||12:27 PM||||Thank you for the replies.  We cleaned out around the IAC motor and it didn't look too bad.  Can I test the ignition module?  Could it be the ECM is bad?  Can the ECM be tested (other than hitting it, as recommended by the local dealer service shop)?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Pyric||12-07-2001||12:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MotoFemme:<BR><B>Thank you for the replies.  We cleaned out around the IAC motor and it didn't look too bad.  Can I test the ignition module?  Could it be the ECM is bad?  Can the ECM be tested (other than hitting it, as recommended by the local dealer service shop)?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I would try EVERYTHING else before the computer.  Chances are... or at least in my experience... that if the computer was causing that, it would cause other problems as well.  <P>Not only that, but something would have had to play a hand in damaging the ECM, and if you plug in a new one without fixing that... you'll fry the new one.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Linc1Tampa||12-09-2001||10:59 AM||||Have you had any luck getting your Fiero running again?  This sounds similar to the problems I am having with my 87 4cyl. I have spent a nice some of money in labor and parts with no success and if it is not running by Jan 1 it will be in the AutoTrader.<BR>Lincoln<BR>87 4cyl (Money pit, will not run)<BR>84 Indy ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||12-09-2001||11:13 AM||||Check the MAT sensor... it's in the DIS article in my cave. Also check the PCV vac line and make sure it's sealled. clamp all joints on that line.(be gentle with the clamps or you'll ruin the elbows.)<P>unplug the transmission electrical connector if it's an automatic. The TCC solinoid can flake out depending on temperature. Usually does it when hot but can do it anytime.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||GTDude||12-11-2001||08:55 AM||||Does the engine only stall when slowing down to a stop or at highway speeds?  I'm GM auto tech....i might be able to help.<P>GTDude<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||MotoFemme||12-11-2001||11:58 AM||||We replaced the MAT sensor on Sunday with no luck - it still stalled after its usual 10 minutes of optimism.  Yesterday it wouldn't start at all, so I'm making some sort of progress, ha!  This is the first time it wouldn't start.  It was fairly cold yesterday, I don't know if that had anything to do with it.  The engine turns over but will not fire (no fuel?).  Also, I could hear the fuel pump running for about 10 or 12 seconds when I turned the key instead of just 1 or 2 seconds as usual.<P>It's a manual shift.  I will try clamping the joints but I wonder if the failure to start gives anyone new ideas.  They are showing the Steeler game out here on Sunday so it would be wonderful to have this figured out by then and avoid the traditional weekend at the Napa Parts Store.||||reg||9
Z||000012||MotoFemme||12-12-2001||01:26 PM||||To Monsieur GTDude -<P>The car is not too picky - it stalls at all (moderate to low speeds.  I don't know if it stalls at highway speeds, as it takes more than 10 minutes to get to the highway and the neighborhood is full of speed traps. Usually the car has already had its crisis by the time we get speeding.<P>We looked at the fuel pump relay and it looks very dirty.  Could this be it?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mark||12-12-2001||07:38 PM||||Moto...<P>In your first post, you stated that that the engine was getting spark during the stall.  Just curious, how do you know that it's hitting on all 4 cylinders correctly?  You indicated that the idle gets very rough and the car bucks. (That sounds like an ignition problem to me).  How is the spark now that the engine won't start at all?<P>After rereading your first post, I don't believe that it is an IAC motor problem, but you did say in a later post that you "cleaned out around the IAC motor".  Just to clarify, I sugested cleaning the idle bypass air path INSIDE the throttle body. This is where deposits will gum up the works and prevent the IAC from responding fast enough to RPM/load changes, etc, which can result in a stall.<P>I'm not sure if the 4 cyl engine is suseceptible to tach filter failures like the V6, but it seems that a faulty tach filter can certainly cause the rough running/stalling. A simple test would be to disconnect the tach filter for a while and see if the engine will run for "more than 10 minutes"!<p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||MotoFemme||12-13-2001||01:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark:<BR><B>Moto...<P>In your first post, you stated that that the engine was getting spark during the stall.  Just curious, how do you know that it's hitting on all 4 cylinders correctly?  <P>**** Mark:  We hooked up a timing light through the passenger compartment.  The firing pattern didn't change through a stall, though we don't know for sure all cylinders are firing correctly ****<P>You indicated that the idle gets very rough and the car bucks. (That sounds like an ignition problem to me).  How is the spark now that the engine won't start at all?<P>**** I haven't tested the spark but we took out the fuel pump relay and the car started today. It was also warmer, and I didn't try it first with the relay in - not a very scientific method ****<P>After rereading your first post, I don't believe that it is an IAC motor problem, but you did say in a later post that you "cleaned out around the IAC motor".  Just to clarify, I sugested cleaning the idle bypass air path INSIDE the throttle body. This is where deposits will gum up the works and prevent the IAC from responding fast enough to RPM/load changes, etc, which can result in a stall.<P>**** Yes, this is what we did ****<P>I'm not sure if the 4 cyl engine is suseceptible to tach filter failures like the V6, but it seems that a faulty tach filter can certainly cause the rough running/stalling. A simple test would be to disconnect the tach filter for a while and see if the engine will run for "more than 10 minutes"!<P>**** What and where is the tach filter? ****<P>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 12-12-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by MotoFemme (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mark||12-13-2001||10:46 PM||||I'm not a 4 cyl owner so I'm certainly not speaking from experience, but here goes.  The tach filter is electrically connected between the coil primary (switched side) and the tachometer as shown here <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero06.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero06.pdf</A> <P>Physically, it is located near the distributor and ignition coil, and looks like an ignition condenser (a term from the good 'ol days). Disconnecting it will disable the tach, but only temporarily (i.e., for the duration of the "test".<P>Actually, I'm not even certain the '88 4 cyl uses a tach flter.  Doesn't it have the DIS ignition (two separate coils?).  Maybe one of the 4 cyl experts can chime in on this one. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mark||12-13-2001||10:48 PM|||| double post<P>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 12-13-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
